From: pear <pear@localhost>
Date: Mon, 17 Sep 2001 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [trainer] test: the transfer queue tests start Ray
 themselves, with the CPUs its actors take

tq.init() creates a controller and two storage units, which take one CPU of Ray's each, and waits until they are up.
The fixtures let it start Ray implicitly, sized by the machine: on a machine with two cores the second storage unit
is never scheduled and tq.init() waits forever. They now start, and shut down, a cluster of three CPUs.
---
 tests/trainer/ppo/v1/test_reissue_inflight_on_cpu.py | 5 +++++
 tests/trainer/ppo/v1/test_replay_buffer_on_cpu.py    | 5 +++++
 2 files changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/tests/trainer/ppo/v1/test_reissue_inflight_on_cpu.py b/tests/trainer/ppo/v1/test_reissue_inflight_on_cpu.py
index fece90c65bb6e24fbedaadfab0da85b3ec6106bc..17a1dd10faed03ed63f74461694030ce4e6e4e4d 100644
--- a/tests/trainer/ppo/v1/test_reissue_inflight_on_cpu.py
+++ b/tests/trainer/ppo/v1/test_reissue_inflight_on_cpu.py
@@ -35,6 +35,7 @@ round-trip that backs checkpoint consistency (matching ``_save_checkpoint``/
 import uuid
 
 import pytest
+import ray
 import torch
 import transfer_queue as tq
 from omegaconf import OmegaConf
@@ -47,9 +48,13 @@ from verl.utils import tensordict_utils as tu
 
 @pytest.fixture(scope="module")
 def tq_init():
+    # A cluster of this module's own, with the CPUs TransferQueue's actors take (the controller and the two storage
+    # units, one each): on a machine with fewer cores, a cluster sized by the machine leaves tq.init() waiting forever.
+    ray.init(num_cpus=3)
     tq.init()
     yield
     tq.close()
+    ray.shutdown()
 
 
 @pytest.fixture
